---
import { Icon } from "astro-icon/components";
import BaseLayout from "../../components/BaseLayout.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

const project = projects[0];
---
<BaseLayout title="Shredder" description={project.description} work={{ type: "CreativeWork" }}>
  <header class="page-hero container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Project / Reconnaissance tooling</p>
    <h1>Shredder</h1>
    <p class="lead">A Python CLI that turns service discovery into a reviewable record of what was observed and what deserves a closer look.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="button button-primary" href={project.repository}>View source <Icon name="lucide:arrow-up-right" /></a>
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="/projects/">All projects</a>
    </div>
  </header>
  <section class="container section content-layout">
    <figure class="project-flow">
      <ol>
        <li>Discover ports</li>
        <li>Fingerprint services</li>
        <li>Collect script evidence</li>
        <li>Rank leads and retain artifacts</li>
      </ol>
      <figcaption>Shredder’s scan and review workflow.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>Initial enumeration produces a mix of open ports, service fingerprints, script results, and raw logs. I built Shredder to keep those artifacts connected so I could trace a lead back to the observation that produced it.</p>
      <h2>Discovery and fingerprinting</h2>
      <p>Shredder uses Nmap for discovery by default, with an optional RustScan backend for TCP discovery. Nmap handles fingerprinting, NSE collection, and the UDP stage. The quick, full, and full-UDP profiles control the breadth of discovery.</p>
      <h2>Evidence and triage</h2>
      <p>The rulebook ranks leads by investigation priority and records the evidence behind each match. The run directory includes structured results, raw scanner artifacts, and checkpoint records. A resumed run can reuse completed stages when their saved inputs and evidence still match.</p>
      <div class="tag-list">{project.stack.map((tag) => <span class="tag">{tag}</span>)}</div>
      <h2>Example triage record</h2>
      <p>In a sample report, a service match records the observed protocol, the source of the match, and the reason it deserves review. The original scanner output remains available alongside the ranked result.</p>
      <h2>Limits</h2>
      <p>Results depend on reachability, the selected profile, and what the scanners observe. Rulebook matches are investigation leads. Review the raw evidence before reporting a vulnerability. Use Shredder on systems you are authorized to assess.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button button-secondary" href={project.repository + "/blob/main/docs/RULEBOOK.md"}>Read the rulebook ↗</a>
        <a class="text-link" href={project.repository + "/releases"}>Releases ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
